Low Growl speaks at the moot:
We are few and our enemies are mighty. We need every advantage we can
get. I know this. The Koranyi sisters are the mightiest Garou warriors we
have, and we cannot afford to lose them.
(Confused responses, from cheers to jeers.)
Still, I won’t lie. I hate the sisters. I hate them. They get their
packmates killed. You insult them, they’ll kill you. And they have. I lost a
friend to their murderous pride.
(The crowd grows quiet.)
Let me tell the story of their sundering. I was there because they
needed a guide. I knew the terrain. For some of us, there’s a temptation
to cheer at this story because it’s a story where those who hurt us fall.
But those cheers are hollow because the only thing this does is take us closer
to the victory of the Wyrm.
We had heard stories of Zhyzhak, the Destroyer, the Chosen Child of
the Wyrm, the mightiest of the Black Spiral Dancers. Her black scourge
could tear the flesh off the most powerful of the ahroun dedicated to
Gaia. Her enemies would fall before the very mention of her name. She
walked the Spiral, the Black Labyrinth, and emerged favored by the Wyrm
as no one before or since.
Yet the Koranyi sisters believed themselves the strongest of all Garou
who love Gaia. Surely, they could, together, vanquish anything the enemy could
muster? Surely the chosen of corruption and decay didn’t stand a chance
against the spirits of storm and thunder?
It was a cold November night in Louisiana, on a decaying oil tanker slowly
leaking onto the waters of the Caribbean. We had heard that Zhyzhak
was here, though for what purpose we couldn’t fathom.
We climbed aboard this rotting giant steel hulk quietly. We heard the
drumming, the uneven beat, the shrieks, the screams and the moans. The
gibbering and the ecstasy of the Spiral. For Zhyzhak wasn’t alone. She’s
Garou too, and Garou have packs.
