We had the drop on them. They were arrogant, drunk, careless. They
believed they owned the night. Unfortunately, so did we.
The first to die was a young pup who idolized the Koranyi sisters. He
barely had time to change into the war form before Zhyzhak snapped her
whip and tore off his skin. She didn’t even have to change before killing her
first warrior of Gaia.
For the sisters she did change. But before she did, I saw her face
alight with rage. She looked the same as Ilona Koranyi when she tore my
friend and packmate into bloody pieces. The rage of the Wyrm, not the
rage of the righteous.
Those who had joined the sisters as packmates died. To their honor,
they tore into the Spirals in Zhyzhak’s company and destroyed many. For
a fleeting moment, I believed we would win. No matter my hatred for
the sisters, when they fight, their claws speak the poetry of Gaia. Always
anticipating each other, always ready to strike and defend, rip and tear.
Their fur slick with the blood of the enemy, their fangs biting into the
flesh of Zhyzhak, the Destroyer, the Child of the Wyrm.
Only, to bite her is to bite the fires of corruption. It is to swallow
the poison flames of the Wyrm itself.
Zhyzhak threw a silver dagger that hit me in the stomach so hard that
I staggered over the side of the ship, certain I’d fought my last fight.
The last thing I saw as I fell was the sight of Karola Koranyi, engulfed
in flames, her howl reduced into a shriek of agony and terror.
Her sister, Ilona, and I were the only survivors of that fight. Ilona
pulled me to the beach where I bled, the silver dagger burning in my guts.
Of her sister, there was no sign. The ship had disappeared into the morning
mists.
Ilona was staring at the sea. In that moment, I could not hate her
for she was lost.
