INTRODUCTION
Introduction
The Garou are not lacking for enemies.
The slavering Black Spiral Dancers hold a
dark mirror to the Garou, showing what
they look like bereft of the guiding hand of Gaia.
The many fronts of the Pentex Group and other
human companies with secret information about the
supernatural realities of the world despoil the face of
Gaia on a grand scale. Banes, spirits of despair and
rot, infest everything, including living beings they
turn into the monstrous fomori.
The Garou are warriors fighting for Gaia. This
book is all about the opposition: the people,
monsters and other entities the Garou seek to stop
and vanquish on and off the battlefield.
The trouble is, so many of the worst enemies of
the Garou are not warriors seeking an honorable
fight. Instead, they’re secretive, malicious liars who
hide in the carcass of humanity. Or worst of all,
they are humanity.
After all, humans have never needed help in
finding ways to fulfill their worst impulses. There’s
nothing as human as shortsighted greed.
The Wyrmtide Rises
Gaia is in her death throes. As
her body thrashes, extreme weather,
earthquakes, and rising sea levels threaten
human habitation. Entire nations risk sinking
below the waves as pieces of the polar ice caps come
tumbling down into the sea.
To the Garou fighting in the Apocalypse,
the Wyrmtide is both a metaphor and a literal
description of what’s happening to the planet. It’s a
reference to the way rising waves throw plastic trash
onto the beaches they’ll soon consume. Everywhere
there seems to be more fomori, more Black Spiral
Dancers, more malignant spirits of destruction.
These, too, are the Wyrmtide, threatening to drown
the world in garbage.
For most Garou, the Wyrmtide is an implacable,
unconquerable enemy. How can anyone fight the
tide? Yet some argue just the opposite. It’s in the
nature of the tide to recede. Most of all, the tide
submits to the moon, and what are the Garou if not
creatures of Luna?
These thoughts may make it seem that the remains
of the shattered Garou Nation can yet win the
war, at least until the grim reality of a Black Spiral
Dancer’s claw tears apart such fancies.
One of the odd side effects of a victorious enemy
is that the minions of the Wyrm fight each other
more than ever before. After the shattering of the
Garou Nation, many Black Spiral Dancers and
privileged executives of Pentex fronts feel that they
have destroyed their old nemesis. Nothing stands in
