Chaos and Stasis
The Wyrmtide makes all problems
worse, even those that have nothing to do
with the Great Destroyer. Spirits of the
Weaver are drawn to humans and organizations
which privilege order above all, often to the
exclusion of sense or safety. Places where rules
are followed, whether they lead to anything good
or not, are often rife with the emanations of the
Weaver, stifling Gaia as bad as any Bane.
Indeed, some Garou, many Red Talons chief
among them, argue that it is the Weaver and not
the Wyrm that represents the real enemy. In their
minds, the Wyrm is in overdrive, driven mad by the
all-encompassing reach of the Weaver. If the Great
Spinner is brought back under control and its webs
burnt and slashed, all may be well again.
For some, such talk leads to dangerous ideas like
seeking the end of human civilization. A few have
even come to the conclusion that the only tribe that
understands the importance of this is the Black
Spiral Dancers. Thus, are defectors made.
The most heartbreaking of all enemies are those
born of the Wyld. Great beasts rampaging in the
dark corners of the world may be animated by
a Wyld-spirit. The chaos of creative force may
emerge in fantastically violent storms raging across
continents, driven by climate change and spiritual
forces drawn from the dawn of time.
Few Garou wish to fight creatures of the Wyld, yet
sometimes they must.
Gaia is not the enemy. She is the battlefield.
Every time we step into the Umbra we hear the Howl.
We see visions of destruction. We smell the acrid stench
of burnt flesh.
Is there anything sweeter? We are winning and the
Umbra sings of our victory.
The Weaver tries to strangle the Wyrm in its webs
but it’s unaware that those very same webs can be used
to transmit poison. Use the tools of the enemy against it.
The Weaver’s webs doing the Wyrm’s work.
