INTRODUCTION
Gaia’s Howl
As the Wyrmtide rises, Gaia drowns
in its filth. This is one of the first things
a Garou notices when stepping into the
Umbra for the first time: Gaia’s Howl resonates
through the spiritscape, the shadow paths vibrating
with her pain and despair.
For many Garou, the Umbra and its many creatures
is what finally convinces them of the immediate
necessity of the war for Gaia. It’s one thing to oppose an
abstraction like climate change. Quite another to actually
witness the spirits of greed and hatred hover over sites of
human carelessness and abuse. Once you have bit down
into the throat of a spirit of radioactivity, you know that
the enemy is out there and you need to destroy it.
And indeed… The enemy truly is out there,
everywhere in the physical world as much as it is in the
depths of the Umbra. Gaia’s Howl resonates through
the moonlit paths and the forgotten byways of the spirit
world. Somewhere in the depths, spirits of pollution,
filth, and destruction respond in a vast Apocalypse
Wave, threatening to turn the Wyrmtide into a deluge.
Chapters
The book consists of six chapters
and two appendices. The goal is to give you
all you need to feature colorful, interesting,
and terrifying enemies in your Werewolf: The
Apocalypse chronicles.
Werewolf is all about the final war to save a dying
world. In these pages, you’ll face the creatures that
seek to accelerate that painful death, wittingly or
not. They are horrifying not only in themselves, but
also because they each represent one way for Gaia to
die and fall into ruin.
Chapter One: Fight To Win looks at the tactics
the Garou employ against their enemies from the
perspective of the many characters who populate the
shattered Nation. The war for Gaia feels impossible
to win, but there is no space for defeatism among the
last of the Garou warriors. You just have to figure
out how to really fight to win. The chapter also
includes new player options and Loresheets.
Chapter Two: Organize for Annihilation looks
at the most dangerous enemies of all: humans.
The ordinary middle manager of a cut-rate energy
company is not much of a threat against a Garou
warrior. The real threat posed by humans lies in
organization. Once assembled into corporations,
NGOs, and other organizations, humans are perfectly
capable of destroying the entire livable biosphere. This
chapter details Pentex fronts and other organizations
serving the Wyrm, wittingly or not.
Chapter Three: The Poison Seeps In reminds us
that the threats faced by the Garou go beyond the
fully human. The Umbra is full of Banes, the fomori
skulk across the earth, and the Black Spiral Dancers
are easily the biggest tribe of Garou around. The rot
of humanity attracts sickness of a supernatural kind.
Chapter Four: Everybody Is Against You is all
about the supernatural enemies spawned by the
Weaver and the Wyld, corrupted to the cause of
the Wyrm. After all, as the world falls ever further
towards the end, spirits who previously served
the cause of the other two great Triatic entities
increasingly turn towards the Destroyer.
Chapter Five: Where To Fight details locations
where the characters can take on all the enemies
presented in this book. Every fight needs a backdrop,
and that is what this chapter is all about. It presents
interesting, fleshed-out locations you can drop into
your game and repurpose as you see fit.
Chapter Six: Stories puts everything you’ve read
about so far into practice. What does it look like
when all the enemies, organizations, and locations
presented in this book are put into use for the
purposes of making a Werewolf story? The chapter
presents story outlines you can use for single session
games or drop into an ongoing chronicle.
Appendix I: Human Enemies considers the
question of how to make human enemies interesting.
Amidst all the Banes and fomori, sometimes it’s the
humble FBI agent who causes the most trouble for
the players’ pack.
Appendix II: Storyteller Options gives the
Storyteller new game mechanical choices to create
more varied enemies such as Banes, fomori, and
Black Spiral Dancers. 
