CHAPTER ONE: FIGHT TO WIN
If loss is inevitable, why fight?
For some Garou, this is the question that causes
them to spiral into harano. They lose the fighting
spirit that has characterized werewolves since time
immemorial and fall into depression. The world is
going to end in a miasma of dust and radiation and
nothing can be done about it.
Many a philodox has taken a different tack in
answering this question. They argue that as long
as there are Garou fighting for Gaia, the war is not
lost. The history of conflict has endless examples of
overconfident armadas falling to their own hubris and
clever underdogs defeating enemies twice their size.
There are ways in which the debased
circumstances of the Garou can be turned into an
advantage. The enemy fights among themselves,
creating openings for those willing to exploit such
rifts. Many servants of the Wyrm have written
off the Garou entirely, making it easier to attack
by surprise. An enemy who believes it has already
achieved victory won’t be so cautious.
This chapter also contains new character options
and Loresheets particularly related to the fight
against the Wyrm.
The Correct Path
There are many opinions among the
Garou about what the best tactics in the war
for Gaia are. Some prefer guerrilla warfare,
while others advocate for cooperation with human
activists. A few believe that any interaction with
humans plays into the hands of the enemy, while
some say that the Garou must use the tools of the
Wyrm to destroy the Wyrm.
There’s little consensus among the tribes of
the shattered Nation. A few views are commonly
proscribed only because they’re so often associated
with specific enemies.
The Cult of Fenris shows what happens when an
entire tribe falls to hauglosk and believes that only
total war and absolute discipline is going to bring
victory. They believe in violence and obedience,
killing Wyrm creatures and Garou of other tribes
in equal measure. If you’re not with them, you’re
against them and deserve to die.
The existence of the Cult has tempered some of the
more extreme views among tribes like the Shadow
Lords and the Red Talons. Few want to be associated
with the tribe that many blame for breaking apart the
Garou Nation in their hubris and Rage.
The existence of the Stargazers has a similar effect
as that of the Cult of Fenris. The Stargazers argue that
the Weaver represents a greater threat than the Wyrm.
More Garou might make the same argument if it
didn’t cause them to be lumped together with another
tribe seen as traitors by many. The Stargazers failed
to stand with other tribes when it mattered and that
means their ideas cannot be trusted.
