WYRMTIDE Fight to Win: FAQ
Here are some of the questions that commonly arise
when the Garou try to figure out ways to fight their war
for Gaia.
How does a werewolf pack feasibly bring down a
midsize company?
Businesses need to be able to function to survive.
Salaries need to be paid, and money has to come in. If
the Garou successfully sabotage the basic functioning
of a business, it eventually has to close.
Many of the Skills a player’s character may have are
very useful for this purpose. You can use Driving to ram
a supply truck into another, causing material losses.
Politics is helpful for the purpose of identifying political
weaknesses that could be exploited, such as company
support for an unpopular politician. Finance helps
analyze the company so that any acts of sabotage
cause maximum damage.
What are the limits of violence?
The Garou are warriors of immense power, but not
every challenge can be met with claw or fang. Yet
surprisingly often, a little judiciously applied violence
goes a long way. Identifying supernatural threats is
key: destroying fomori, banishing Banes, and killing
Black Spiral Dancers typically results in a much
cleaner environment, and human allies can often take
it from there.
Survival can be used to fell a tree to block a road
so human reinforcements won’t be able to arrive in
time to help a paramilitary squad of fomori. This way,
battlefield control helps keep loss of life to a minimum.
Intimidation is another useful Skill. Threaten a group
of Black Spiral Dancers and their human sycophants,
and soon only the werewolves remain. Faced with the
Garou, humans have a tendency to run.
How do you recognize a supernatural threat?
Garou have killed innocent humans, believing them
to be fomori, more than once. This is why checking
what things look like in the Umbra, trying to provoke
supernatural manifestations, and listening to the
advice of trusted spirits are all worthwhile methods.
Better to avoid repeating the mistakes of the past.
Gifts like Penumbral Senses and Sense the True
Form are obviously useful, and others like Sharpened
Senses, Shadow Sense, and Sight from Beyond may
also have their applications in the right circumstances.
How to find human allies?
A key problem for many Garou is that potential
human allies, i.e., humans who share goals similar
to the veneration for Gaia, often reject violence.
For this reason, the most successful tactic may
be anonymity. The Garou can keep a popular,
ecologically minded politician alive despite the best
efforts of an Albion Petroleum hit squad to kill him
without having to reveal themselves. Just figure out
which humans fight the good fight and make sure
they can keep fighting.
That said, a solid human identity is very helpful
for interacting with potential allies. Backgrounds
like Contacts, Fame, and Mask can be leveraged
effectively in the right circumstances.
Many different types of humans can be useful in
the right circumstances. Here are a few examples.
Community Activist: Ever since all that fracking
started, tapwater in town has been undrinkable.
The Community Activist is a middle-aged woman,
a schoolteacher, and the impact of the fracking on
the community has pushed her to the brink and
beyond. She can help keeping an eye on the actions
of the company on the ground and find information
through bureaucratic means. She can also speak
to the community, for whatever ends the players’
characters might decide.
Disillusioned Soldier: For a while, the Disillusioned
Soldier believed in the mission, but eventually the
glaring contradictions of the military life became too
much. He left the navy and fell to drinking for a while.
Eventually he decided that he had to get a grip. He
needed something to believe in. He needed to be
useful. Thus, he volunteered as crew on ships run by
eco activist organizations, the kind engaged in direct
action on the high seas. The Soldier is useful if you
need somebody to take you to an Albion Petroleum
drilling platform on a small speedboat, ideally
without anybody noticing.
Whistleblower: A true believer, the Whistleblower
works for Living Earth Logistics. They were attracted
by all the talk about sustainability and working for
shipping solutions that respect the biosphere. It was
a real shock to them to realize that the company’s
ecological mission is a sham and that underneath
the facade something awful was festering. The
Whistleblower is a clean-cut, credible professional,
useful for damaging their employer, as long as
the players’ characters manage to thwart the
assassination attempts that are sure to come.
