CHAPTER THREE: THE POISON SEEPS IN
The privilege and the tragedy of the
Garou is to be able to see the spiritual corruption
plaguing the world in tangible terms. A werewolf
can go to the Umbra and bite a spirit. Banes and
Black Spiral Dancers show that the ravaging of Gaia is
something concrete, not just a metaphor.
This is the fact that convinces so many newly
changed werewolves that the war for Gaia is worth
fighting for. They have their first experiences
with Banes and decide that these monstrosities
must be opposed.
The forces of the Wyrm are legion, all the
more dangerous because they share no central
organization or identity. Many, perhaps even most,
don’t even think of themselves as creatures of the
Wyrm, but rather follow their own agendas.
A Bane of smog wants to suffocate and is happy
to do so whether that serves the Wyrm or not. A
Wyrm cultist or a Black Spiral Dancer might believe
in a cosmology recognizable to the Garou, but their
motivations and goals are still their own.
Banes
Banes are legion. The word is
used by the Garou to denote any spirit
they see as affiliated with the Wyrm,
and they are not in short supply.
Millions of Banes flock to feast on
the human species and their countless
emotions. When the people of the human
world lose hope and allow despair to hold
them down, Banes find a way to hook into
their very being.
In a way, humans and Garou have more
in common with these spirits than one might
assume at first. A Bane is essentially the
distilled expression of the very same selfdestructive
drive that has led to the Apocalypse.
They fulfill their purpose without any fantasies of
free will that humans like to cling to, sometimes
even after taking a Bane into their souls and
becoming fomori.
As with most spirits, it’s not within the nature of
a Bane to stop. Banes simply perform the task that
they embody, surging to their victims like moths
to a light. Often, a Bane’s work is noticed too late,
and the victim finds themselves succumbing to
urges that nourish the spirit in question. Seemingly
normal behavior begins to feed an eternally hungry
spirit of entropy.
To the Garou, the most effective way to deal with a
Bane is often to just prevent them from being created
in the first place. Some Banes are transformed from
a stock of regular spirits. A lot of old legends claim
that the Wyrm’s servants kidnap spirits to defile and
debase them. In fact, it is more often the gradual
destruction and corruption of the Mother that
twists spirits enough to become Banes.
Furthermore, what constitutes a Bane is not as
clear-cut as some Garou would like. After all, many
spirits are manifestations of natural feelings or
concepts. A storm-spirit may be entirely normal and
78
WYRMTIDE
natural, or it can be a Bane spewing poison rain. The
Garou tend to consider spirits of rot and decay as
Banes regardless of the fact that these are perfectly
naturally occurring phenomena.
In some rare cases, stopping a spirit from completely
turning into a Bane depends on solving the situation
that is gradually destroying them or even repairing the
damage to the spiritual world that the spirit depends
on. More often than not, the cost is too high, and the
Garou end up destroying the spirit to avoid bolstering
the ranks of their enemy. If done soon enough, it
might not even reform as a Bane.
The biggest issue arises from the belief that
if humans cause Banes to form, thus increasing
manifestations of the Wyrm’s power, why are the
Garou not killing as many of them as possible? It
seems like the most obvious solution.
The truth is, the Garou have once tried doing
exactly that. It was called the Impergium, and
even then, the Wyrm was rising. After all, such a
campaign of death also tends to create the conditions
for more Banes to manifest.
It is the pain of those who live, the imbalance and
horror that feeds the Banes, and perhaps it was the
Impergium itself that broke the balance of the world.
No one really knows, but all suffer the consequences.
Storytelling Banes
The challenge with using Banes in your chronicle as
the Storyteller is to make them individual without
taking away from their strangeness. This is why
it’s good to remember that they shouldn’t just be
cannon fodder but should have motivations and
goals of their own.
Here are some sample motivations.
Ambition: The Bane wishes to enter the physical
world because it believes that it’ll have great
opportunities to fulfill its nature there.
Recognition: The Bane is vain and wishes to
be recognized for its efforts. A clever Garou can
manipulate it by speaking to its ego.
Ideology: A fervent believer in the same taxonomy
of spirits the Garou use, the Bane knows it serves the
Wyrm and wishes to destroy the world.
Fear: The Bane believes that the Garou are about
to destroy it and wants to destroy them first.
Giving the Bane a recognizable motivation makes
it easier to present them as individuals distinct from
each other.
What Is a Bane?
The Garou themselves are often divided when it
comes to the nature of Banes. After all, what spirit is a
Bane and what is just nature being nature with blood
dripping around its muzzle? Some consider a spirit’s
negative effect on their surroundings as their primary
qualifier, while others have specific criteria that a Bane
needs to possess before it can be deemed a Bane.
Even acts of possession are not a surefire way to
categorize a spirit as a Bane. In the end, it comes
down to the subjective views of the pack.
The Cult of Fenris is so engrossed in their crusade
for eternal war against the modern world and the
Wyrm, they are willing to destroy neutral spirits of
time, death, and decomposition just to be certain
they leave no enemy.
Different septs all teach their cubs a score of
various signs that the Garou consider indication that
a spirit is a Bane — the following are just a few of
