Worshiping Banes
It is not surprising when Banes find a place among
humans as objects of worship. In their attempt
to find any sort of meaning, many humans have
reached out and found a Bane that would be more
than willing to grant blessings to pliable humans.
Others have made voluntary ties with a spirit, only
for it to reveal its true nature once the worshipers are
enthralled and cannot bear to separate themselves
from their personal deity.
Societal pressure, shame, and harm are like candy
to the children of the Wyrm. Religion can become
the perfect excuse for humans to exploit other
humans, and as soon as that pattern begins, the
Banes begin to nourish themselves.
The Triatic Wyrm
The occult mythology of the Wyrm
mirrors that of the Triat. Black Spiral Dancer
theurges often invoke an aspect of the Triatic
Wyrm, one of the three most commonly referenced
aspects of the Destroyer. The Wyrm has as many
aspects as there are supplicants willing to invent
them, but the three of the Triatic Wyrm are the
ones often considered to be fundamentally and
cosmologically true.
The Triatic Wyrm may be an attempt to make
the Destroyer more understandable in the sense
that its manifestations are easier to visualize. Still,
nobody ever meets an aspect of the Triatic Wyrm.
They’re Umbral archetypes, too vast to be seen or
comprehended.
Wyrm of Calamity, Beast-of-War
Some things don’t need to be complicated. The
Beast-of-War represents the Wyrm in its most basic
form, as an immensely powerful destructive force
barreling through the world and demolishing
everything in its wake. It’s associated with the
fundamental power of the Wyld and worshiped by
Black Spiral Dancers who revel in violence and pain.
Many of them say that the Rage of the Garou is itself
a manifestation of the Beast-of-War, although Garou
from the tribes of Gaia don’t like such talk.
The Beast-of-War manifests in many shapes in
the world, or perhaps it’s the other way around
and it’s fueled by many of the most violent
phenomena in existence. When mighty empires
bomb the struggling citizens of poor countries
and drunken men beat up their families, the
Beast-of-War rejoices.
Wyrm of Consumption, Eater-of-Souls
We want. We want to consume, we want to own
and we want to destroy. Nothing is ever enough
for us. There’s always more, and we need to have
it, no matter what it does to us. Eater-of-Souls
represents the empty hole in the center of the
Wyrm, the endless need driving it forward. It’s
associated with the Weaver and its desire to bring
everything under control and worshiped by Black
Spiral Dancer philodox and ragabash who want to
have everything.
The Eater-of-Souls is the spirit of capitalism, the
idea of endless growth, acquisition, and wealth. When
greed is good, the Wyrm of Consumption smiles.
Wyrm of Corruption, the Defiler Wyrm
The urge to annihilate is a product of the self.
The Defiler is the Wyrm’s image of itself, a mirror
turned into its primal core, and by extension to
each of our souls. The Wyrm is us, and the Defiler
just makes that explicit. It’s the most intelligent
and powerful of the Triatic Wyrms, capable of
justifying everything to itself and to you. Every
act of sadism is an act of mercy, every cruelty for
people’s own good. The Defiler is worshiped by
Black Spiral Dancer galliards and theurges as they
make sense of the Spiral.
The Defiler is present everywhere humans exist,
pervading the entire world. It’s there when we allow
ourselves to be vindictive and cruel, when we justify
our sordid actions as righteous and good. The urge
to power is at the core of the Defiler, when humans
compel their brethren to act against their will.
