In Stories:
The Strontium Howl are an extremely alarming
phenomenon to any member of the shattered Garou
Nation. They are often seen as a sign of things
to come, a taste of a future world in which the
structures of the Weaver have completely collapsed,
leaving behind a Wyrm-controlled hellscape of
mutual death and degradation.
The Veil Must Be Preserved: A chapter of the
Howl has rolled into the region, vandalizing human
settlements and looting businesses indiscriminately.
It won’t be long before human authorities crack
down hard. If the cops are not enough, the army
will be called in. They may believe they’re only
dealing with a domestic insurrection, but that
doesn’t mean they’ll pull their punches. Were this to
happen, the Veil would be history, since Black Spiral
Dancers openly fight against heavily armed humans,
Delirium or no. The players’ pack must both destroy
the Howl and hide any trace of their supernatural
nature in order to keep the secret from leaking.
The Greatest Machine: All the Strontium Howl
chapters in the region are coming together to build
a giant Strontium Engine at a local junkyard, big
enough to belch tar into the sky day and night. They
believe that the Engine requires the continuous
sacrifice of humans to feed it, pushing people into
the fire in their hectic, nightly Rites. To make matters
worse, the machine’s power echoes in the Umbra,
summoning the Banes of primordial conflagrations.
Genius for Destruction: Looking through the
corpses of dead Howl, the players’ pack discovers a
Talisman of unusual power and utility, a weapon
that could be used in the war for Gaia. It raises many
questions: Should it be destroyed, or is it okay to
use the tools of the Wyrm against it? Is the spirit
bound in the Talisman a Bane, and if yes, so what?
And, most of all, who made the Talisman, and where
can they be found? The pack may wish to kill the
Strontium theurge responsible for creating such
weapons, but they might also be tempted to try to
get such a genius to defect.
Deathtalons
You need discipline, rigor, and obedience to become
truly dangerous. The Deathtalons are not well
known among the Garou of the shattered Nation
because they don’t leave many survivors. They travel
in small, well-coordinated packs and strike hard
and fast, only stopping to mutilate the bodies of
their enemies according to their arcane Rites. Many
Garou have witnessed their handiwork but have
unwisely written it off as typical Black Spiral Dancer
monstrousness.
And indeed, the Deathtalons are monstrous,
but in a different way than many of their brethren.
Other Black Spiral Dancers often denigrate them
for being servants of the Weaver in disguise,
taking all the talk of discipline to mean that the
Deathtalons seek to order the world into a shape
that’s more to their liking.
This is not the case. To the Deathtalons, the world
is irredeemably tainted. Most Black Spiral Dancers
believe in the idea that time is cyclical and the
world must be destroyed so it can be created anew
as a pristine paradise. The Deathtalons take this
more seriously than most, believing that it is their
sacred duty to foment mass destruction in any shape
possible, and to do so requires organization.
The Deathtalons are a paramilitary cult, seemingly
rigorous yet guided by the dreams and nightmares
emanating from the Black Spiral. Although their
individual packs appear disciplined, the higher one
113
CHAPTER THREE: THE POISON SEEPS IN
looks at their hierarchy, the stranger it appears. The
leadership is constantly in flux as older, more experienced
Deathtalons disappear on pilgrimages to the depths of
the Umbra or seek wisdom in self-destruction.
The cult preaches ego death for its members,
the acceptance of the fact that each warrior of the
Apocalypse must sacrifice their lives for the cause. Each
leader is just a mouthpiece for the emanations coming
from strange spirit entities. This is the paradox of the
Deathtalons: they’re a disciplined organization built
around a core of roiling, nihilistic chaos.
The sense of mission and spiritual connectedness
enjoyed by the Deathtalons makes them attractive
to a certain type of Garou who defects to the Black
Spirals but still wants to feel like they’re doing the
right thing. The cult’s martial culture makes a
former Cult of Fenris or Shadow Lord Garou feel
like they’re still fighting for a just cause and not just
giving in to harano.
The Deathtalons look down upon all other Black
Spiral Dancers, considering them to be dilettantes
who don’t understand the true spiritual grandeur of
the Wyrm. In return, many Spirals see the way the
cult seeks to monopolize the tribe’s entire ideology
and faith as offensive.
Patron Spirits
Despite the martial nature of the cult, the Patron
Spirits of individual Deathtalon packs are often
mystical and conceptual, philosophical rather than
warlike. Spirits of greed and annihilation, hatred and
loss are more common than more tangible entities.
A Deathtalon may wax poetic on matters of the
spirit world, expounding on the beauty of cyclical
time and the glory of rebirth, even as they carve the
symbol of the Bat onto the forehead of a prisoner
with a knife.
Personalities
Mystics, seekers, and warriors are attracted to the
Deathtalons in equal measure. It’s the cult for high
achievers, serial killers, and those with idiosyncratic
ideas about their place in the world. It works best for
those of a serious disposition and a powerful ability
to withstand pain.
Janet the Accountant
Not an actual accountant, Janet got her nickname
from her habit of counting her victims. For each one
she killed, she marked her skin with another small
scar to continue the great spiral now decorating her
entire body. She is experiencing a spiritual crisis
because she’s run out of space: adding more scars
would detract from the elegance of her scar spiral.
Is this a sign from the Wyrm that Janet must
stop killing? Or does her desire to keep her scars
aesthetically consistent represent an affectation she
must get rid of? Should she walk into the Umbra and
never return?
Janet is a galliard and an accomplished murderer
and likes to flay her victims in imaginative ways in
tribute to her spirit patrons. Her crisis means that
she’s refrained from killing recently, but it’s likely
that one moment of Rage is enough to push her to
inflicting yet more death.
