In Stories
Deathtalons are a serious, dangerous enemy rarely
encountered by chance. A typical Deathtalon pack
operates according to a clearly defined plan based
on solid intelligence, striking hard and fast and then
disappearing into the night. If the Deathtalons have
any weakness, it’s to be found in their spiritual side.
Always keen to follow their visions, they are capable
of changing their plans quickly if they believe that
this is the message they receive from beyond.
You Are the Prey: The local septs have become
used to fighting disorganized, overconfident Black
Spiral Dancers who rarely bother with strategy or
tactics. Individually strong, their packs are riven
with strife, making them inefficient when fighting
against the local veteran Garou. Suddenly all that
changes when the bodies of local theurges are
found one by one, each with their eyes gouged out.
Worryingly, one of the players’ pack is also a theurge.
Something’s Not Right: A strange pack has
arrived at the local moot. They appear spartan
in their rigor, completely separated from human
life. Yet nobody can make heads or tails of their
philosophy because they refuse to explain themselves
except in allegory. Most take them for Stargazers and
argue whether they should be kicked out, but the
reality is something far worse: they’re Deathtalons
seeking to find a specific Garou pup whose face
they’ve seen in a vision. What to do when the players’
characters realize their modest sept plays host to a
pack of experienced Deathtalon warriors?
Chain of Command: A local ragabash elder has
come up with an ambitious plan to force the Black
Spiral Dancers to fight each other. They want the
characters to travel deep into the Umbra and parley
with the spirits, convincing powerful Banes to send
visions to a Deathtalon hive that they must purge the
heretics from those who have danced the Spiral. But
what do the Banes want in return for such a favor?
Poison Spawn
You’re in a meeting with the ad agency and it feels
like something is wrong. Their people are aggressive,
arrogant, and predatory, using a lot of words like
dominate, destroy, and conquer. But this is what you
hired them for, you reason, so the deal goes on.
According to the Poison Spawn, the only way for
the Black Spiral Dancers to successfully serve the
Wyrm is together with their greatest ally: humanity.
The Spawn seek to hold onto their human lives,
each living a double existence. By day, they’re the
same as every other human, working to make
a living. By night, they’re cultists of the Wyrm,
giving everything they have to their superiors in the
hierarchy in the belief that this is what pleases the
Great Destroyer the most.
The Poison Spawn is a tightly controlled cult
that sends out its members to make money and
gather resources among human society. The cult’s
central leadership is in Nashville, Tennessee, with
individual chapters in different cities and regions
all subordinate to the hierarchy. There have been
a number of schisms and civil wars among the
Spawn in the past, some successfully taking over the
organization. Yet despite this chaos, it persists, as it
has for over a century, making it one of the longestlasting
cults in the changeable world of the Black
Spiral Dancers.
Each member of the Spawn is marked with the
symbol of the Bat, typically branded or tattooed
in a painful Rite when they go through their
initiation into the cult. Spawn are expected to dress
conservatively, and the cult pays an inordinate
amount of attention to policing the appearance of
its members. The sex lives and private relationships
of the Spawn are also tightly controlled, and this
control is often abused.
115
CHAPTER THREE: THE POISON SEEPS IN
This is why the Spawn is one of the Black Spiral
cults with the highest amount of defectors to
other tribes. When such a defection occurs, Spawn
members are directed to spare no effort in killing
such an apostate. This happens even when a member
defects to one of the other Black Spiral cults.
The cult encourages its members to channel all
their repressed urges into their work. At human
workplaces, they tend to be intense, argumentative,
and feared. If they reach leadership positions, they
like to amass personal power through bullying and
violence. The cult particularly values members
who make it in finance, politics, media, or the
entertainment industry. They’re the best educated
Black Spiral cult, by far.
The Poison Spawn believe that they are the real
Black Spiral Dancers. They have the numbers, the
organization, and the dedication to prove it. The
Spiral belongs to them, and everyone who purports
to be of the tribe but doesn’t accept a place in the
organization is a heretic. This attitude means that
most other Dancers hate them with a passion.
Patron Spirits
The Poison Spawn are dedicated to Bat. Other
Patron Spirits are only acceptable if they’re in a clear
subordinate role to the tribal patron. Following
other Patron Spirits is seen as heretical, something
that’s punished by torture or death.
One way members or even entire packs leave the
cult is by forming a relationship with a new spirit
who often allows for more freedom.
Personalities
Outwardly, Poison Spawn are often some of the most
normal, well-adjusted Black Spiral Dancers out there.
They have jobs, families, educations, and retirement
plans. They live in houses, drive cars, and take their
kids to play baseball. Because of this, they’re often
the most difficult to detect and root out compared to
other, more extravagant Black Spiral cults.
Cronus “The Wolf Eater” Jones and Francisca
“Poison Angel” Jones
The married couple leading the Poison Spawn,
Cronus and Francisca
Jones are in their fifties and
live in a luxurious Tennessee
estate serving as the cult’s central
hive. Cronus is a theurge and a spiritual visionary,
conveying the wishes of the Destroyer from beyond,
while Francisca is a philodox and keeps the cult
functioning in terms of human society, making sure
that the whole corrupt edifice won’t collapse due to an
inopportune FBI investigation.
The Joneses preach self-control and sacrifice but
live lives of excess and indulgence. They believe that
they’re entitled to exploit their followers, and indeed
anyone, as much as they like in the service of the
Apocalypse. Sometimes life at their estate devolves
into a bloody, torrid haze for weeks on end as the
Spawn dance the Spiral.
Although the couple appears harmonious as they
lead the Spawn in their vile Rites, they represent one
of the cult’s religious fault lines. Francisca is happy
to incorporate Christian imagery into the spiritual
practices of the Spawn, while Cronus believes it
makes them weak.
