In Stories:
The horror of the Poison Spawn is similar in some
ways to the horror of the Garou of other tribes,
living hidden among humanity yet subject to a
terrifying Rage that destroys their relationships.
Many Garou assume that the Black Spiral Dancers are
too monstrous to successfully live among humans,
but that’s not really true. The misconception arises
because the Dancers most often encountered by the
Garou are the ones who fit in the least.
Closer Than You Think: How do you recognize
a Black Spiral Dancer? From their maddened howls,
their matted fur, the venomous gleam in their eyes?
For many Black Spirals, that’s true. But this time, the
players’ pack discovers that the enemy is much closer
than they believed possible. It’s the CEO of the ad
agency where one of their Touchstones works, a local
cop, or even a schoolteacher. What to do when the
enemy has burrowed so deeply into human society?
Hand in Hand: Human enemies like malignant
corporations are immensely destructive, but at
least the people running them are soft targets for
a Garou warrior. Even if such an enemy is infested
with fomori, it’s still manageable for a strong pack.
But this time, things are different. The unassuming
middle manager is a Black Spiral Dancer, and so is
the chief of security.
Rescuing a Friend: There are fates worse than
harano. A septmate of the players’ characters has
joined a Moon Cult, or so they believe. But this cult
seems more malignant than the others, and soon
the characters realize it’s a cult of the Spiral. They
need to rescue their friend before it’s too late, but to
do that they need to go deep into the world of the
Poison Spawn. Is infiltration an option?
Malcontents
The children of the Black Spiral are a
varied lot, and it’s impossible to force all of
them to follow the tenets of any single group.
117
CHAPTER THREE: THE POISON SEEPS IN
Many Dancers accept no larger organization than
the pack, the sept at most. They have their own
idiosyncratic ideas and values and follow them as
they best see fit.
There is a wide range of werewolves that run
among the Black Spiral Dancers. The one thing
they all have in common is their acceptance of
being monsters who want to bring about the end
of the world. The argument that the Dancers make
tends to be that the world must be destroyed for
a better world to be born. However, there’s no
shortage of individual Dancers for whom that is
just an excuse to give in to their worst impulses.
Rage can be very seductive when you’re convinced
it’s for the greater good.
In a Werewolf chronicle, the Black Spiral Dancers
serve as a dark mirror of the Garou. They use their
Rage and their immense capacity for violence in the
service of a great goal. It’s just that their goal is to get
to the end of the world as fast as possible, while the
Garou still dream of saving Gaia.
The Black Spiral Dancers lie to everyone, even
themselves. In a chronicle, the Dancers are the best
option for a Storyteller to tempt the players’ pack
with the promise of giving into their Rage without
having to think about the consequences. They
present an enemy who can meet the Garou on equal
footing. It is a chance to have long-term foes just
enough like the Garou for it to be uncomfortable.
Converts to the Darkness
A frighteningly large number of Black Spiral
Dancers are converts from other tribes. There are
many reasons why Garou would join up willingly
with the Spiral Dancers, but the most common are
fear and fatigue: fear of one day losing everything
they hold dear and the fatigue of fighting a battle
that already seems lost. Wouldn’t it be nicer to be on
the winning side? Maybe the Spirals are right. When
harano is already clawing at your soul, the idea that
the Black Spiral Dancers are what a werewolf is
actually supposed to be isn’t that much of a stretch.
Some Garou are tempted by the lure of the
forbidden truths about our world: How do we
know we’re supposed to be serving Gaia anyway? Is
Gaia even real? Aren’t werewolves supposed to be
monsters? And why is it that we are not allowed to
eat humans? Are we not the apex predators? Their
flesh smells so tasty!
There are countless ways to fall and begin the
dance. Once you do, you’ll never dance alone.
Abby Last Scream: The Lost Judge
Abby First Howl walked the path from start to
finish. A leader. A Galestalker. Then a Forsworn. At
the end, a Black Spiral Dancer.
Her old pack never understood her distress —all of
them wanted to help her get better from her harano.
It was not until she was lured to the Spiral that she
understood. It was foolish to resist. Depression is a
sign that there’s something wrong with where you
are in the world. Once you change your position,
things align and you’ll feel better.
The only truth left was the unmaking of
everything. Fighting against that was too much.
Abby Last Scream emerged from the Spiral as a
changed werewolf, a Half-Moon Black Spiral Dancer
seeking to usher the Apocalypse upon the rest of the
world. Only then can we be at peace. Abby hates that
she has become what she is, but she hates what the
world has doomed her to become even more.
