CHAPTER FIVE: WHERE TO FIGHT
Every epic fight needs a cool
backdrop. This chapter details a series of
locations you can use and adapt in your own
stories. Their purpose is going to depend on why
the players’ pack is there and what their enemies
want, but in each case, whatever action ensues
should be interesting.
Each of the enemy types detailed in this book
suggests its own locations. Albion Petroleum has
oil rigs, refineries, and gas stations. Black Spiral
Dancers have festering Hives where they come
together to perform their vile Rites. In each case,
an interesting location is going to bring the enemies
to life and, if the scene turns to combat, presents
new tactical options.
Washington County Jail
The jail is a twenty-minute drive
outside the county’s largest city. It consists of
two buildings and a yard inside two walls. The
smaller building has inmate processing and shortterm
incarceration, the larger three-floor structure
has the medical ward and cells.
The difference between a state prison and a county
jail is that this is a place where arrested people are
kept while they are waiting for a trial or are serving a
shorter prison sentence. County jails also often hold
people when state prisons are too overcrowded.
The jail is a worn, austere compound with thick,
red brick walls. It currently has 230 inmates, a
little more than its official capacity. The security
arrangements are strict: the place has cameras,
locked and monitored gates and doors, and
precise procedures for everything. The jail has an
atmosphere of boredom, stress, anxiety, and fear.
