and level of assessed danger. You’re taken to a cell,
which you share with one to five people. The cells are
small, as clean as you bother to keep them, and often
windowless. The cell has steel toilet bowl and a sink,
bunk beds, and not much else.
Depending on the crime, you can get out of jail on
bail, or a good lawyer can speed up the process. Bail
money and lawyer’s fees can be expensive. Usually bail
varies from a few hundred dollars (minor misdemeanor)
to tens of thousands (serious violent felony). A defense
lawyer’s hourly rates are hundreds of dollars, depending
on their experience and the nature of your crime.
It’s possible to visit an inmate, but visitors must
book their visit beforehand and be approved for
visiting. Visiting hours are limited to only one hour
per week and for only two visitors at a time. You
can’t bring any gifts or items for the inmate, and
there is also a dress code for visitors: for example, no
jewelry except wedding rings, no gang symbols or
attire that might be interpreted as such, no garments
that expose chest or back, no outerwear, and shoes
must be kept on all times.
The jail offers relatively little for inmates to
do. The compound has a small library, whose
books have been received as donations. There’s a
recreational area with a basketball court, exercise
equipment, and space to walk.
The prison system is
always on the side of the
