WYRMT IDE The Garou have plenty of supernatural
enemies, from the Black Spiral Dancers to the
vast host of Banes congregating in the Umbra.
Yet sometimes, the most dangerous despoilers of the
Earth are ordinary humans.
This appendix is all about how to make human
enemies interesting. Not everybody needs to have
supernatural abilities to present a real danger to
the Garou, their Touchstones, or the causes they
care about.
The chapter Allies and Antagonists in Werewolf (p.
239) presents the basics of how antagonists are built.
Traits
The Garou have Gifts and Banes and
fomori have powers of their own. With
human enemies, the challenge lies in giving
them interesting Traits that are not supernatural in
character. Here are some examples of how you can
approach this question:
Gear: What kind of gear does the antagonist
have? Handguns and rifles give bonuses to damage.
Some enemies may have silver bullets or knives, if
they’re preparing to go against a werewolf. Armor
makes humans more durable. Gear can be found in
Werewolf, p. 195.
Reinforcements: Many military or paramilitary
enemies, as well as those with significant resources
backing them up, have access to reinforcements.
This can be made into a Trait whereby the
antagonist has the ability to call in a squad of
Acheron Security Services mercenaries, for example.
Environment: Many antagonists occur in specific
environments which may provide inspiration
for interesting Traits. For example, perhaps the
antagonist gains a two-dice bonus to all tests or the
Difficulties against them increase by 1 when they’re
on home ground with prepared defenses.
Collateral Damage: The antagonist purposefully
exploits the moral standards of the players’
characters. As an example, an antagonist can have
a Trait which allows them once per turn to grab a
bystander (if available) when attacked, so they take
incoming damage instead.
Social Abilities: The Difficulties related to
seeing through the antagonist’s lies are unusually
high, or they’re able to convince bystanders of their
viewpoint if allowed to speak.
Influence: The antagonist has influence of some
sort. Perhaps they have the ability to ferret out
Touchstones or call in police raids on the players’
characters’ human allies.
Delirium: A simple option is to grant the human
antagonist immunity to Delirium. However,
to keep things more interesting, there are other
options as well. Perhaps they’ve been treated by
an experimental drug which always prompts an
aggressive reaction when Delirium is triggered.
Tactics: The antagonist favors specific tactics which
grant them an advantage. For example, a sniper could
always engage from an elevated position so they can get in
a shot or two before the players’ characters reach them.
Weakness: Not every Trait has to be positive.
Sometimes a character can have an interesting
weakness. For example, they’re a coward and all
Intimidation tests against them gain a one-die bonus.
