APPENDIX I: HUMAN ENEMIES
Organizations
The enemies of the Garou are not
limited to the despoilers of the earth. There
are organizations ranging from government
agencies to secret conspiracies that cause problems
for the werewolves for reasons of their own.
The Arcanum
An academic society of scholars
interested in the occult with some actual
supernatural truth hidden in their vaults,
the Arcanum — or, more specifically, each of its
members and chapterhouses — is interested in
collecting and codifying knowledge of the occult,
including the Garou and the spirits they deal with.
Unlike other adversarial groups, the Arcanum, as a
whole, isn’t focused on any return on investment.
They collect — some say hoard — knowledge of the
supernatural world. What their end goal may be other
than filling their libraries and vaults is anyone’s guess.
The Arcanum has its origins stretching back to at
least the Victorian era, and its structure has changed
little since then. Chapterhouses are set up in areas with
major supernatural activity. Small, local colonies are set
up in a region to collect as much information as possible
on any unnatural activity, past or present. These may
eventually become a full chapterhouse of their own
rather than reporting back to a full chapterhouse.
Smaller, temporary gatherings of arcanists are referred
to as lodges, often convening for a specific research
endeavor. For the Garou, arcanists come in where the
Arcanum has a particular interest in major Banes, longlived
fomori, Talismans, other shifters, and caerns.
Arcanum chapterhouses tend to be found in old
urban centers where they were purchased with old
money and passed down over the generations to their
current members. New chapterhouses and colonies
are sometimes founded with new money from rich
investors. These new chapterhouses employ a small
staff of active researchers and librarians, who are
tasked with compiling and organizing supernatural
discoveries, as well as sending missives to their
neighboring or governing branches.
Arcanum agents become more dangerous the longer
they remain at large. Many understand that places of
power (what Garou call caerns), even those that have
lost most or all of their spiritual energies, are defended
by spirits or shifters. They understand that some
folklore holds truth even when unpredictable; with
time to study and request reports from their network
of chapterhouses, they will be prepared for a second
encounter should they survive the first.
A Garou -to-Arcanum Dictionary
Any conversation with an Arcanum agent is filled with terms the Garou likely find unfamiliar,
and coming to an agreement on what a caern is may take more time than the Garou have
patience for. Below are a few sample terms. Storytellers should get inventive as each
Arcanum agent may have a different set of preferred terms, each more pretentious than
the last. Many argents rely on Greek or Latinate scientific and pseudoscientific terms, while
others draw heavily upon traditional occult terms, or terms from the local language of the
culture the agent is currently studying or infatuated with.
Garou Term Arcanum Term(s)
Caern Place of power, nexus, ley convergence
Spirit Supernal being, Nephal/Nephilim, spirit, ghost
Garou Werewolf, lycanthrope
Other Shifters Therianthropes
Talisman Artifact, relic, spirit-prison, item of power
Fomor Possessed
