out for the Garou simply because the werewolves
are so powerful. Doing so, however, tends to
cause problems, either due to occult artifacts
and information being distributed in the local
community or other chapterhouses investigating the
disappearance of their colleagues.
Those Garou with experience dealing with the
Arcanum tend to suggest scaring them off, but that
knowledge isn’t commonly passed down in the Age
of Apocalypse.
Project Twilight
By the time a pack knows they’ve
attracted the attention of these government
boogeymen, it’s in the form of a JSOC kill
team loaded for bear (and Garou, for that matter).
Investigations into their activities hit a wall of
conspiracy theories: they’re both in every branch
and agency of the U.S. Federal Government (and
some even claim every government from Russia
to Monaco) and don’t exist at all. They’re your
neighbor, sanitation worker, and every other
harmless stereotype waiting for you to slip up.
In short, they’re shrouded in the paranoia and
propaganda that other anti-werewolf groups would
kill for — and many have.
The truth is stranger than fiction, like in the case
of the black book operation that its own director
publicly shut down. Its agents spread to the winds
into what is now a global decentralized operation,
albeit still a tiny one. There are only roughly 700
active agents across the globe, leaving any given
Garou more likely to get killed by a cow than to run
afoul of a Project Twilight agent. That said, those are
active agents compared to magnitudes more inactive
agents who’ve likely done one or two stints for Project
Twilight, furthering their goal to gain leverage and
power over anything that isn’t strictly human and
keep its influence out of human governments.
This goal attracts plenty, from patriots and bigots
to skeptics and believers, who gather intelligence
for Project Twilight often as part of duties to their
particular agency, whichever acronym comes out in a
spoon of alphabet soup. Project Twilight operatives
nearly exclusively come from these agencies or other
arms of government, such as former military, political
analysts, and anyone else who can go to sleep knowing
they’re serving their county against threats that its
citizens would never believe stalk among them.
Project Twilight agents often use tried-and-true
spy techniques and technology, usually more analog
than expected, to avoid cyber-attacks and leaving
a digital footprint. A single operative with a rifle,
a powerful scope, and a silver bullet is more likely
to execute their militant actions than a kill team.
Still, militant action isn’t how the organization
gets the work done — it’s less about extermination
than gaining information and ousting supernatural
influence from the workings of governments, one
day and operative at a time.
Garou are most likely to come across a Project
Twilight operative in government offices, embassies,
and other bastions of bureaucracy where rage and
passions go to die.
Personalities
Special Agent Ekaterina DeLorme
Ekaterina DeLorme has traveled the world as a proud
member of the U.S. Central Intelligence Agency,
adopting personalities, developing assets, and
hunting down some of the worst terrorists known
to man… and some of the creatures that man only
experiences in nightmares.
Beneath the mask that’s remained stoic in the
face of assassinations, enhanced interrogations, and
far worse is the soul of a patriot, growing heavier
and heavier under the growing burden of lies and
trauma. A veteran, she’s a handler for the agents
that come and go, blissfully ignorant of their work
to keep America safe — putting her at the center
of local Project Twilight operations and one of the
heads that can’t be regrown so quickly.
