Stories
Project Twilight is a reminder that many of the
things the Garou do are massively illegal. Generally
speaking, society frowns on murder.
Sudden Strike: It all happened so fast, and no one
spotted the sniper before there were splattered brains
all over everything. United by anger and loss, a pack
doggedly hunts down their suspect to find they’re a
squeaky-clean government employee. Does the pack
follow the Rage in their hearts even when it’s leading
them to a perfectly normal human whose death may
wake the sleeping hydra, or can they make something
more of this situation while maintaining the Veil?
Twilight Mystery: Local Garou find two bodies
at the edge of the caern’s territory, both in cheap
suits, armed with the weapons that killed one
another, and with different agency badges. The plot
thickens when their cars are found nearby, full of
surveillance equipment and encrypted reports on
paper, a wealth of information if they can decipher
the codes. The former Project Twilight operatives
couldn’t trust one another and left a treasure trove
on the pack’s doorstep. Can the pack unravel the
mystery and stay one step ahead of someone like
Special Agent DeLorme sent to clean up the mess?
Fall
Project Twilight’s strength of being a decentralized
entity with agents across the FBI, CIA, NSA,
CDC, GSG-9, Interpol, and many more agencies
can also be its greatest weakness, as those loyal to
their agency compete for information and honors
rather than cooperate. With different handlers
and agendas, operations can run contradictory to
one another, spreading paranoia and friendly fire
as every person an agent might trust could be a
monster in disguise.
Project Twilight’s domain is entirely human, leaving
them vulnerable to supernatural powers that can wipe
out their agents or disrupt analog communications
and records. Agents who wash out are either killed
or left so broken that they’re left out to pasture,
where a kindly Bone Gnawer can trade half a meal of
scrounged food for stories too wild to take seriously.
Of course, another way to deal with Project
Twilight is to set them loose on your enemies, as long
as you can keep your fingerprints clean. A photo of a
local Congress member with a Fenris Cultist or some
leech’s lackey that ends up in the right place can turn
on a self-cleaning oven for the enemies of Gaia. 
